c-programming-topic-3-m3-q1.explanation:
P1 
 int a = 5; // global 
 int main() { 
 int a = 7; // local 
 } 
 Local variable a hides the global a. 
 This is allowed in C. 
 So P1 compiles. 
 P2 
 int main() { 
 int a = 5; 
 int a = 7; 
 } 
 Here both declarations are in the same scope. 
 You cannot declare the same variable twice in the 
same block. 
 So this gives a compilation error. 
 P3 
 int main() { 
 int a = 5; 
 float a = 7; 
 } 
 Again, two variables with the same name in the same 
scope. 
 This is also a compilation error. 
 Therefore, only P1 compiles without error. 
 Correct answer:  (A) Only P1 will compile without 
any error

compiler-design-topic-4-m4-q14.explanation:
15. ANSWER:- (8)  
 We will need one temporary variable for storing the 
result of every binary operation as Static Single 
Assignment implies the variable cannot be repeated 
on left hand side of assignment. 
  The given expression: 
 q+r/3+s−t∗5+u∗v/w 
 t1=r/3; 
 t2=t∗5; 
 t3=u∗v; 
 t4=t3/w; 
 t5=q+t1; 
 t6=t5+s; 
 t7=t6−t2; 
 t8=t7+t4; 
 So in total we need 8 temporary variables. If it was 
not mentioned as a static single assignment then the 
answer would have been 3 because we can re -use 
the same temporary variable several times.
